WEBVTT

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:17.110
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass, and this is our 500th episode. And what does that feel like? Well, it feels like both a milestone and it feels like nothing. It fills like an odometer clicking over.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥 WBEZ 电台的《美国生活》，由公共广播国际公司发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯，这是我们的第 500 期节目。感觉如何？嗯，它既像是一个里程碑，又感觉什么都不是。就像里程表又跳了一格一样。

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:26.110
<v Ira Glass>I was talking to the show's senior producer, Julie Snyder, about this. She's been here for 15 of the show's 17 years, since episode number 58.
<v Ira Glass>我曾与节目的高级制作人朱莉·斯奈德谈过这件事。她在我们节目十七年的历史中待了十五年，从第 58 期开始就在这里了。

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:31.120
<v Julie Snyder>Five hun-- what-- I just-- I just come to work. I do my job. I go home.
<v Julie Snyder>五百……什么？我只是……我只是来上班。做好我的工作。然后回家。

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.360
<v Ira Glass>Play each ballgame the best I can.
<v Ira Glass>尽我所能打好每一场比赛。

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:33.830
<v Julie Snyder>Yeah, exactly.
<v Julie Snyder>没错，就是这样。

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:34.760
<v Julie Snyder>[LAUGHTER]
<v Julie Snyder>[笑声]

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.000
<v Ira Glass>No, it really is a blur.
<v Ira Glass>不，这真的就像是一场模糊的记忆。

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:59.710
<v Ira Glass>It is a blur. And over the last few weeks here at the radio show, we talked about what we should do for the 500th episode. And like, first of all, should we mark it at all? You know what I mean? Like 500 shows on the radio actually isn't that big of a deal for most programs. Like Terry Gross, she knocks through 500 shows like every two years. Doesn't even notice. Just spins right by them. Lots of shows are like that.
<v Ira Glass>确实很模糊。过去几周，我们一直在讨论第 500 期节目该做些什么。首先，我们真的需要纪念它吗？你懂我的意思吗？在大多数电台节目中，500 期其实并不是什么大不了的事。像特里·格罗斯，她可能每两年就做满 500 期了。她甚至都不会注意到。就这样过去了。很多节目都是这样。

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:16.800
<v Ira Glass>But, you know, the kind of show that we do here, it takes three or four months to make each episode. So that is different. And besides that, you know, 500, the number 500-- that is a number you notice. And if we didn't do something today, like that would feel weird, you know?
<v Ira Glass>但是，你知道，我们做的这种节目，每一期都要花三四个月来制作。所以这不一样。而且除此之外，500 这个数字——这是一个你会注意到的数字。如果我们今天不做点什么，那感觉会很奇怪，你懂吗？

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:38.090
<v Ira Glass>So anyway, so as we talked about different things that we could do for this episode, at some point, Julie had the idea that it might be fun to go back to the archives and choose favorite moments from the last 499 episodes. And all this hour, you're going to be hearing the producers of the program, the people who find the stories and who put them together each week, talking about their very favorite moments that we have ever put on the radio.
<v Ira Glass>总之，当我们讨论这期节目能做些什么时，朱莉有个想法，或许回到档案库，选出过去 499 期里最喜欢的时刻会很有趣。在这一小时里，你将听到节目的制作人，也就是每周寻找故事并将它们编排在一起的人，讲述他们有史以来在电台播放过的最喜欢的时刻。

00:01:38.090 --> 00:02:00.540
<v Ira Glass>And the reasons that the producers picked their favorites, I think, are different than the reasons that most listeners would give. Some of them chose stories that had been, I have to say, completely forgotten by the rest of the staff for years. Some chose just, like, one little section of script. Some chose, like, a scene that secretly made them cry and they never told the rest of us. They said things like this.
<v Ira Glass>我认为，制作人们选择这些片段的理由，与大多数听众给出的理由并不相同。有些人选择了那些我必须说，已经被其他同事遗忘了多年的故事。有些人只选了一小段台词。有些人选了一个让他们私下流泪但从未告诉过我们的场景。他们说了这样的话。

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:04.440
<v Male Speaker>When I heard that, I was like, wow, that's one articulate bank robber.
<v Male Speaker>当我听到那个的时候，我就想，哇，那真是一个能言善辩的银行劫匪。

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:09.370
<v Female Speaker>And then as it unfolds, you just realize, like, no, no, no, no, no, no, no.
<v Female Speaker>然后随着故事的展开，你意识到，不，不，不，不，不，不，不。

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:14.520
<v Male Speaker>I felt like I'd been hit by a car or something. But in a nice way. [LAUGHS]
<v Male Speaker>我感觉就像被车撞了一样，或者类似的感觉。但那种感觉还不错。[笑]

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:22.890
<v Female Speaker>It just made me really mad. Do you remember this? (STAMMERING)  It's mind-blowing.
<v Female Speaker>它真的让我很生气。你还记得这个吗？（结巴）这简直让人大开眼界。

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:28.110
<v Ira Glass>And so I am really excited to play you the stuff that we have found. And let's just jump in.
<v Ira Glass>所以我非常兴奋能为你播放我们找到的这些片段。让我们直接开始吧。

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:41.580
<v Ira Glass>Julie Snyder, our senior producer, is the mastermind behind a lot of our most ambitious shows, like Harper High School episodes, if you've heard those. And she came into the studio with a list of favorite stories from over the years, starting with a shortie.
<v Ira Glass>我们的高级制作人朱莉·斯奈德是我们许多宏大节目的幕后操盘手，比如如果你听过的话，《哈珀高中》那几期。她带着一份多年来最喜欢的节目列表走进录音室，从一个简短的开始。

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:42.910
<v Julie Snyder>It's only like a minute long.
<v Julie Snyder>它只有一分钟长。

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:43.630
<v Ira Glass>OK.
<v Ira Glass>好的。

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:54.700
<v Julie Snyder>It's really, really lovely writing that I have thought about ever since it aired, I don't know, 12 years ago, 14 years ago.
<v Julie Snyder>那是一段非常非常优美的文字，自从它在大约 12 年前、14 年前播出以来，我就一直记在心里。

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:56.580
<v Ira Glass>Wow. What in the world are you talking about?
<v Ira Glass>哇。你到底在说什么？

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:01.970
<v Julie Snyder>It's from Sarah Vowell's Trail of Tears story. Can you guess it?
<v Julie Snyder>来自莎拉·沃威尔的《泪之径》故事。你能猜到吗？

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:03.800
<v Ira Glass>Hold on.
<v Ira Glass>等等。

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:05.240
<v Julie Snyder>That's a whole show.
<v Julie Snyder>那可是整整一期节目。

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:09.600
<v Ira Glass>Like America is a country that hits you but-- that's not the moment.
<v Ira Glass>比如“美国是一个会重创你的国家”，但……那不是那一刻。

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:10.500
<v Julie Snyder>It is.
<v Julie Snyder>就是那一刻。

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:11.290
<v Ira Glass>It is?
<v Ira Glass>是吗？

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:12.320
<v Julie Snyder>It is.
<v Julie Snyder>是的。

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:16.380
<v Ira Glass>I can't remember the set-up for it. I just remember the punchline of it.
<v Ira Glass>我不记得它的铺垫了。我只记得它的点睛之笔。

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:32.240
<v Julie Snyder>Well, the set-up-- OK. Well, so the story, right, is that Sarah is part Cherokee. And she and her twin sister Amy go on this road trip, going from Georgia driving to Oklahoma, following the Trail of Tears. And--
<v Julie Snyder>好吧，铺垫是这样的……好吧，故事是说莎拉是切罗基人。她和她的双胞胎妹妹艾米进行了一次公路旅行，从佐治亚州开车到俄克拉荷马州，沿着“泪之径”。然后……

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:34.310
<v Ira Glass>That their ancestors had walked before them.
<v Ira Glass>那是她们的祖先曾走过的路。

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:52.820
<v Julie Snyder>Exactly. That their ancestors have walked when the Cherokee were expelled from Georgia. And it's super depressing. I mean, what would a trip like that be? It is. It's just depressing. So it's very hard to know what to do with all these feelings. And so that's where her writing picks up. You want to hear it?
<v Julie Snyder>没错。那是切罗基人被逐出佐治亚州时，她们的祖先走过的路。那非常令人沮丧。我是说，那样的旅行会是什么样？确实如此。非常令人沮丧。所以很难知道该如何处理这些感受。这就是她那段文字的切入点。你想听听吗？

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:53.270
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>想。

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:57.020
<v Julie Snyder>OK. It's "Trail of Tears," starting at 45:14.
<v Julie Snyder>好的。那是《泪之径》，从 45 分 14 秒开始。

00:03:57.020 --> 00:03:58.890
<v Ira Glass>OK, here we go.
<v Ira Glass>好的，我们开始。

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:13.360
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - CHUCK BERRY, "BACK IN THE USA"]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 查克·贝里，《回到美国》]

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:41.420
<v Sarah Vowell>The most happiness I find on the trip is when we're in the car and I can blare the Chuck Berry tape I brought. We drive the trail where thousands died, and I listen to music and think, what are we supposed to do with the grizzly past? I feel a righteous anger and bitterness about every historical fact of what the American nation did to the Cherokee. But at the same time, I'm an entirely American creature. I'm in love with this song and the country that gave birth to it.
<v Sarah Vowell>在这次旅行中，我最快乐的时候是我们在车里，我能大声播放我带来的查克·贝里的磁带。我们开在成千上万人死去的路上，我听着音乐想，我们该如何面对这段残酷的过去？我对美国民族对切罗基人所做的一切历史事实感到一种正义的愤怒和痛苦。但同时，我是一个彻头彻尾的美国生物。我爱这首歌，也爱这个孕育了它的国家。

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:58.230
<v Sarah Vowell>[MUSIC - CHUCK BERRY, "BACK IN THE USA"]
<v Sarah Vowell>[音乐 - 查克·贝里，《回到美国》]

00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:19.910
<v Ira Glass>Listening to "Back in the USA" while driving the Trail of Tears, I turn it over and over in my head. It's a good country. It's a bad country. Good country. Bad country. And of course, it's both. When I think about my relationship with America, I feel like a battered wife. Yeah, he knocks me around a lot, but boy, he sure can dance.
<v Ira Glass>在沿着“泪之径”开车时听着《回到美国》，我在脑海里一遍遍地思索。这是一个好国家。这是一个坏国家。好国家。坏国家。当然，两者皆是。当我想起我与美国的关系时，我感觉自己像个受虐的妻子。是的，他经常打我，但天哪，他确实很会跳舞。

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:29.510
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - CHUCK BERRY, "BACK IN THE USA"]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 查克·贝里，《回到美国》]

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:30.430
<v Julie Snyder>Isn't that so nice?
<v Julie Snyder>这不是很美好吗？

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:31.180
<v Ira Glass>It's perfect.
<v Ira Glass>太完美了。

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:46.670
<v Julie Snyder>I know. I remember-- I remember when she wrote it.  I remember reading it. I remember when it aired. I just thought it was great. I love that writing. And I think about it all the time. I think about it for so many times when trying to put stuff-- like how do you hold two thoughts together?
<v Julie Snyder>我知道。我记得……我记得她写这段话的时候。我记得读过它。我记得它播出的时候。我只是觉得它太棒了。我喜欢那种文字。我无时无刻不在想它。当试图把某些东西组合在一起时，我经常会想起它——比如你如何同时持有两种想法？

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:47.580
<v Ira Glass>You mean about this country?
<v Ira Glass>你是说关于这个国家的想法？

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.630
<v Julie Snyder>Yes. All right, you want another one?
<v Julie Snyder>是的。好了，还要再来一个吗？

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:50.010
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:51.800
<v Julie Snyder>OK, you want to do "Testosterone"?
<v Julie Snyder>好的，你想听《睾酮》那一期吗？

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:52.340
<v Ira Glass>Sure.
<v Ira Glass>当然。

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:59.140
<v Julie Snyder>All right. I really do-- so this is another one. I'll just continue to do these through how they personally affected me.
<v Julie Snyder>好吧。我真的很……所以这是另一个。我会继续根据它们对我的个人影响来介绍这些。

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:00.950
<v Ira Glass>Good.
<v Ira Glass>好的。

00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:14.050
<v Julie Snyder>So "Testosterone," this is the one where I feel so bad for you guys. I feel really bad for men. This blew my mind, the interview that Alex did with Griffin Hansbury.
<v Julie Snyder>那么，《睾酮》，这是我为你们感到非常抱歉的一期。我真的很为男性感到同情。这让我大开眼界，亚历克斯对格里芬·汉斯伯里的采访。

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:15.000
<v Ira Glass>Griffin Hansbury.
<v Ira Glass>格里芬·汉斯伯里。

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:29.770
<v Julie Snyder>Yeah. And so Griffin was talking to Alex about-- Griffin was born a woman and went to Bryn Mawr, and was a feminist, and really-- you know what I mean?
<v Julie Snyder>是的。格里芬在和亚历克斯谈论……格里芬生来是女性，去了布林茅尔学院，曾是女权主义者，而且真的……你懂我的意思吗？

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:30.410
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, lesbian feminist.
<v Ira Glass>是的，女同性恋女权主义者。

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:42.050
<v Julie Snyder>Lesbian feminist. And then so then, Griffin decided to transition, and so he started taking testosterone. And he started taking pretty intense amounts, apparently.
<v Julie Snyder>女同性恋女权主义者。然后，格里芬决定变性，所以他开始服用睾酮。显然，他开始服用的剂量相当大。

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:44.040
<v Ira Glass>Like way more than a man has.
<v Ira Glass>比如比普通男性多得多。

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:56.820
<v Julie Snyder>Yeah, it's like, I think, twice. And so this part of the interview is when Griffin is telling Alex about the effects of the testosterone.
<v Julie Snyder>是的，我想是两倍。采访的这一部分是格里芬向亚历克斯讲述服用睾酮后的效果。

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:24.090
<v Griffin Hansbury>The most overwhelming feeling is the incredible increase in libido and change in the way that I perceived women and the way I thought about sex. Before testosterone, I would be riding the subway, which is the traditional hotbed of lust in the city. And I would see a woman on the subway, and I would think, she's attractive. I'd like to meet her.
<v Griffin Hansbury>最强烈的感受是性欲的极大增加，以及我对女性感知的改变，还有我思考性的方式。服用睾酮之前，我会坐地铁，那是城市里传统的欲望温床。我会看到一个女性，我会想，她很有吸引力。我想认识她。

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:33.940
<v Griffin Hansbury>What's that book she's reading? I could talk to her. This is what I would say. There would be a narrative. There would be this stream of language. It would be very verbal.
<v Griffin Hansbury>她在读什么书？我可以和她谈谈。这就是我会说的话。会有一个叙事过程。会有语言流。那会是非常言语化的。

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:58.640
<v Griffin Hansbury>After testosterone, there was no narrative. There was no language, whatsoever. It was just, I would see a woman who was attractive-- or not attractive.  She might have an attractive quality, nice ankles or something, and the rest of her would be fairly unappealing to me. But that was enough to basically just flood my mind with aggressive pornographic images, just one after another.
<v Griffin Hansbury>服用睾酮后，没有了叙事。完全没有语言。只是，我会看到一个有吸引力的女性——或者没那么有吸引力。她可能有一个迷人的特质，比如漂亮的脚踝之类的，而她身体的其他部分对我来说相当没有吸引力。但这足以让我脑子里充斥着侵略性的色情图像，一个接一个。

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:09.650
<v Griffin Hansbury>It was like being in a pornographic movie house in my mind. And I couldn't turn it off. I mean, I could not turn it off. Everything I looked at, everything I touched turned to sex.
<v Griffin Hansbury>就像我的大脑里住进了一家色情电影院。我无法关掉它。我是说，我根本无法关掉它。我看着的一切，我触摸的一切都变成了性。

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:13.180
<v Alex Blumberg>What did you do with that? I mean, what did you think?
<v Alex Blumberg>你对此做了什么？我是说，你怎么想？

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:34.020
<v Griffin Hansbury>Well, I felt-- I felt like a monster, a lot of the time. And it made me understand men. It made me understand adolescent boys, a lot. Suddenly, hair sprouting, and I'm turning into this beast. And I would kind of-- I would really kind of berate myself for it.
<v Griffin Hansbury>嗯，我感觉……我感觉自己像个怪物，很多时候。这让我理解了男性。这让我非常理解青春期的男孩。突然间，毛发滋生，我变成了这种野兽。我会有点……我真的会因此而责骂自己。

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:46.320
<v Griffin Hansbury>I remember walking up Fifth Avenue, and there was a woman walking in front of me. And she was wearing this little skirt and this little top. And I was looking at her ass. And I kept saying to myself, don't look at it, don't look at it. And I kept looking at it.
<v Griffin Hansbury>我记得走在第五大道上，前面有个女人走着。她穿着小裙子和小上衣。我一直在盯着她的屁股。我一直对自己说，别看，别看。但我一直看着它。

00:08:46.320 --> 00:09:09.170
<v Griffin Hansbury>And I walked past her, and this voice in my head kept saying, turn around to look at her breasts. Turn around, turn around, turn around. And my feminist female background kept saying, don't you dare, you pig. Don't turn around. And you know, I fought myself for a whole block, and then I turned around and checked her out.
<v Griffin Hansbury>我从她身边走过，头脑里的这个声音一直在说，回头看看她的胸部。回头，回头，回头。而我女权主义的女性背景一直在说，你这头猪，别回头。你知道，我和自己斗争了一整条街，然后我还是回头看了她一眼。

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:27.770
<v Griffin Hansbury>And before, it was cool. When I would do a poetry reading, I would get up and I would read these poems about, you know, women on the street. And I was a butch dyke, and that was very, very cutting edge, and that was very sexy and raw. And now I'm just a jerk. You know?
<v Griffin Hansbury>以前，这很酷。当我做诗歌朗诵时，我会站起来朗诵这些关于……你知道，街上女性的诗。那时我是个硬汉拉拉，那非常非常前卫，那非常性感且原始。而现在我只是个混蛋。你知道吗？

